    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Bamboo is a remarkable material, prized for its strength, fine upright texture and relaxed, natural look. Although it is technically a grass rather than a wood, it behaves much like a hardwood when used outdoors, and it has a good strength-to-weight ratio. However, because it is a natural material, it is affected by the weather. Without care, the fresh greens and tans of a new bamboo fence fade to a weathered grey, and damp can lead to mould, rot and splitting.</p>
<p>A simple seasonal routine helps your bamboo fence or screen keep its looks and strength for as long as possible. This guide covers what to do each spring, summer, autumn and winter, plus the structural checks and refinishing that sit alongside them. Always read the care guidance from your fence supplier and the label of any cleaner or finish you use, as products differ.</p>

<h2 id="spring">1. Spring: clean and reseal</h2>
<p>Spring is arguably the most important time for bamboo maintenance. After a damp, cold winter, the fence needs a thorough look and a fresh start, and the main goal is to remove any mould or mildew that has taken hold.</p>
<p>Walk the whole length of the fence and look for black or green spots. Bamboo contains natural sugars and starches that can encourage fungi when moisture is trapped against the surface. Often a soft brush and a bucket of water with a little mild washing-up liquid is enough. For more stubborn mould, use a mould and mildew cleaner suitable for bamboo, or a mild solution tested on a hidden spot first, used as the label directs. Avoid harsh bleach or ammonia cleaners, as they can discolour bamboo and dry out its surface.</p>
<p>Once the fence is clean, let it dry thoroughly, as the product label advises, before you check the finish. If water no longer beads on the surface, the protective coat has worn away and it is time to reapply. Spring is a good moment to do it, so the fence is protected before heavy rain and the stronger summer sun. Use a finish made for bamboo or exotic timbers, ideally one with UV protection, and reseal as the product label advises; sunnier or wetter spots will usually need it sooner.</p>
<p>Many sealers and finishes contain mildewcides. Wear gloves, work in good ventilation, keep drips off plants and lawns, and never rinse brushes or leftover product into drains.</p>

<h2 id="summer">2. Summer: sun and splitting</h2>
<p>As temperatures rise, the main threat shifts from damp to strong sun and drying out. The sun's bleaching effect is the most common cause of a tired-looking fence: without UV protection, the surface fibres break down and turn silvery grey. Some people like the weathered look, but heavy greying can also be a sign that the surface is becoming dry and brittle.</p>
<p>A sound, UV-protective finish applied in spring does most of the work. In a very hot, dry spell, it is enough to keep an eye on the fence; there is rarely any need to water it.</p>
<p>Summer is also when "checking", or vertical splitting, is most likely. Bamboo canes are hollow, and as they lose moisture the outer wall shrinks faster than the inside, which opens up vertical cracks. Most of these are cosmetic. If you find a large split, especially near the top of a cane, a little exterior-grade wood glue or clear outdoor sealant can help stop water getting into the hollow centre. Capping the tops of upright poles does the same job.</p>
<p>If the fence stands near a swimming pool or by the sea, rinse it regularly with fresh water to wash off chlorine splash or salt spray before they build up on the finish.</p>

<!--figure:sunlit-bamboo-wall-shadows|Strong summer sun fades bamboo and dries its surface, which is when fine cracks open up.-->

<h2 id="autumn">3. Autumn: clear the base and check ties</h2>
<p>Autumn is the season of preparation. The aim is to make sure the fence and its surroundings do not hold moisture as the weather turns cold and wet.</p>
<p>The most common mistake is letting leaves, mulch or grass clippings pile up against the base of the fence. Damp debris acts as a bridge that lets water wick into the bottom of the canes, and over time this leads to rot at ground level that can weaken a whole panel. Keep a gap of about 5–8 cm (2–3 in) between the bottom of the bamboo and the soil or any mulch, and rake debris away from the fence line as part of your autumn tidy-up.</p>
<p>Check the ties and wires as well. If your bamboo is held together with galvanised wire or twine, look for sagging, fraying or rust, and tighten or replace loose ties before winter winds arrive. If climbers or vines grow over the fence, trim them back: they look lovely, but they hold humidity against the canes and make cleaning and sealing much harder.</p>
<p>If your bamboo is a screening roll fixed over an existing fence, check the fence underneath too. Rolls can hide a rotting timber post, a rusting wire or a leaning section, and the roll is only as secure as whatever it is tied to.</p>

<!--figure:weathered-canes-wire-ties|Autumn is the time to check ties and wires before winter winds test them.-->

<h2 id="winter">4. Winter: snow, salt and water</h2>
<p>Winter brings freezing temperatures and plenty of moisture. Bamboo is fairly hardy, but repeated freezing and thawing can widen existing cracks.</p>
<p>Snow is the main winter job. Heavy snow piled against the fence can make panels lean or canes bow, so brush it off gently with a soft broom after a heavy fall, and do not shovel snow up against the fence when you clear paths. Keep metal shovels away from the base, as a knock to cold, brittle bamboo can chip or crack it.</p>
<p>If de-icing salt is used on nearby paths, driveways or roads, salt spray can damage the finish. Rinse the fence with fresh water during a milder spell to wash off the residue, and avoid spreading rock salt right next to it. On a fence beside a gritted road, a temporary hessian (burlap) screen over winter is an optional extra layer of protection. Finally, check that gutters and downpipes are not sending a steady stream of water onto the fence, as constant soaking will eventually cause rot.</p>

<h2 id="structural-checks">5. Structural checks</h2>
<p>The canes need seasonal care, but the frame that holds them is just as important. Give the structure a full check at least in spring and autumn.</p>
<p>Start with the support posts. Most bamboo fences are fixed to treated timber or metal posts. Make sure each post is upright and does not move in its footing. A loose post lets the panels catch the wind like a sail, which puts a lot of strain on the fixings. If a post set in concrete is loose, it usually needs resetting, which is a job for a fencer if you are not confident doing it yourself.</p>
<p>Then look at the fixings: screws, wires and brackets. Use stainless steel or galvanised hardware outdoors. If you see rust streaks running down the bamboo, the hardware is failing and should be replaced promptly. Replacing a rusty screw takes minutes; replacing a panel that has rotted behind a rusty bracket is a far bigger job.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Combine the post and fixings check with your spring and autumn cleans. You are already walking the fence line with a brush, so a quick push on each post and a look at each bracket adds only a few minutes.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="refinishing">6. Refinishing</h2>
<p>From time to time a bamboo fence needs a full refinish rather than a quick touch-up, especially once the colour has faded unevenly or the old finish has started to flake. How often depends on your climate, how much sun and rain the fence gets and the product you use, so follow the finish manufacturer's guidance.</p>
<p>The choice of finish matters. Bamboo's dense, waxy surface doesn't soak up finishes easily, so use a finish made for bamboo or exotic timbers; general-purpose products can sit on the surface and peel. Tinted finishes give more UV protection than clear ones, which mainly keep out moisture, so a natural or lightly tinted shade helps stop the bamboo turning grey.</p>
<p>To prepare, clean the fence and let it dry fully. If you use a bamboo or timber brightener, or lightly sand rough or flaking areas, wear a dust mask and gloves and follow the product label. Be gentle: heavy sanding or aggressive scrubbing can damage the outer skin of the bamboo, which is what gives it much of its natural protection. Apply the finish in thin, even coats along the length of the canes, treating both sides of each panel where you can reach them so they don't warp, wipe off any excess, and avoid over-applying, as a heavy coat can stay tacky and collect dust and pollen. As with any finish, wear gloves, keep the area ventilated, and keep it off plants and out of drains.</p>

<!--figure:new-golden-bamboo-fence|A fresh breathable finish brings back bamboo’s warm colour and protects it for another season.-->

<h2 id="summary">7. Seasonal checklist at a glance</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spring:</strong> clean off mould and mildew, let the fence dry, check whether water still beads and reseal if not.</li>
<li><strong>Summer:</strong> watch for greying and large splits, seal or cap open canes, rinse off pool or sea spray.</li>
<li><strong>Autumn:</strong> clear debris from the base, keep a 5–8 cm (2–3 in) gap above the ground, check ties and the fence beneath any rolls.</li>
<li><strong>Winter:</strong> brush off heavy snow, keep shovels and salt away, rinse off salt residue, check gutters and downpipes.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>How long does a bamboo fence last?</h3>
<p>It depends on the quality of the bamboo, how it was installed, your climate and how well it is maintained. Fences kept clear of wet soil and refinished regularly last far longer than neglected ones. Screening rolls are lighter and generally need replacing sooner than a framed bamboo fence.</p>
<h3>Can I use a pressure washer on my bamboo fence?</h3>
<p>It is best avoided. High-pressure water can tear the outer fibres and force moisture into the canes, which encourages rot. A garden hose with a spray nozzle and a soft brush is safer and usually enough.</p>
<h3>Why is my bamboo fence turning grey?</h3>
<p>Sunlight breaks down the natural colour of the surface, much as it does with cedar or other timber left unfinished. You can often refresh the colour with a bamboo brightener, used as the label directs, followed by a tinted UV-protective finish.</p>
<h3>My bamboo fence has vertical cracks. Should I be worried?</h3>
<p>Usually not. Vertical cracks, known as checking, are a natural feature of bamboo as it expands and contracts with changes in temperature and humidity. They rarely affect the strength of the fence, but sealing large splits and capping open tops keeps water out.</p>
<h3>Do I need to seal both sides of the fence?</h3>
<p>Yes, where you can reach both sides. Sealing the front and back helps the canes take on and lose moisture evenly, which reduces the risk of panels warping or leaning. If the back faces a neighbour's garden, ask before working from their side.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
